Parsing FASTA 🧬
with open("data/sequences.fasta") as f:
    for line in f:
        line = line.strip()
        if line.startswith("&gt;"):
            print("HEADER:", line)
        else:
            print("SEQUENCE of length", len(line))

This is the same logic as grep "&gt;" — but now you can compute with it.

Building a dictionary from a file
sequences = {}
name = None

with open("data/sequences.fasta") as f:
    for line in f:
        line = line.strip()
        if line.startswith("&gt;"):
            name = line[1:]          # drop the '&gt;'
            sequences[name] = ""
        else:
            sequences[name] += line  # sequences can span many lines!

print(len(sequences), "sequences loaded")

Walk through it line by line. This is the most complex code so far — take your time.

if / elif / else
gc = 0.62

if gc &gt; 0.6:
    print("GC rich")
elif gc &lt; 0.4:
    print("AT rich")
else:
    print("Balanced")

Python checks top to bottom and stops at the first true condition.

Combining conditions
if length &gt; 100 and gc &gt; 0.5:
    print("Long and GC rich")

if seq.startswith("ATG") or seq.startswith("GTG"):
    print("Possible start codon")

if not seq.startswith("&gt;"):
    print("This is a sequence line, not a header")

and · or · not — they read like English 🙂

Exercise 1: Predict, then run 🔮
⏱️ 10 min · chat first
seq = "ATGCCCTAA"

if len(seq) &gt; 5:
    print("A")
elif len(seq) &gt; 3:
    print("B")
else:
    print("C")

if seq.startswith("ATG") and seq.endswith("TAA"):
    print("D")
Which letters get printed? 🤔

25–35 min. Key insight: only ONE branch of if/elif/else runs, but the second if is independent.

Counting with dictionaries 🧮
The classic bioinformatics pattern:
seq = "ATGCGCATG"
counts = {}

for base in seq:
    if base in counts:
        counts[base] = counts[base] + 1
    else:
        counts[base] = 1

print(counts)   # {'A': 2, 'T': 2, 'G': 3, 'C': 2}

Walk through the first three iterations slowly, on the slide, base by base.

Looping over a string too 🧬
for base in "ATGC":
    print(base)

count = 0
for base in "ATGCGC":
    if base == "G":
        count = count + 1
print(count)

The accumulator pattern (start at 0, add inside the loop) is the single most important pattern this week.
